   claim:
     "`akasha/infrastructure/package.json` is absent because a container folder is not a package, and creating one would have been the wrong answer to a question that only looked like a gap. The `workspace-package` page type carries the rule Earn The Manifest — make a folder a workspace package only when a tool must read what it needs — and `akasha/infrastructure/`, `akasha/alan/` and `akasha/browser/` each hold only sub-packages and loose `*.domain.ts` pages, with no loose code at the top. Code carried out of `tools/lib` toward `akasha/infrastructure/` lands in one of its 26 sub-packages, or in a new sub-package folder rooted by its own `*.workspace-package.ts` page. The five `tools/lib/benchmark-*.ts` files needed no new manifest at all: `@akasha/ci-benchmark` already existed and they already imported it.",
   evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-03 while carrying the five `tools/lib/benchmark-*.ts` files in.\\n\\nTHE RULE. `akasha/code-system/workspace-packages/workspace-package.page-type.ts` lines 56-66 carry a directive of kind `rule` named Earn The Manifest, act 'Make a folder a workspace package only when a tool must read what it needs', warrant 'A manifest costs upkeep on every change and buys nothing until something reads it', and among its aids 'Importing it by name is not a tool reading it'. Line 15 has `extendsSlug: 'page-type/domain'` and the invariant at 36-38 reads 'A domain becomes a workspace package by stating a manifest', so what decides is which page is at the folder root, not how deep the folder is.\\n\\nTHE COUNTS. `akasha/infrastructure/` is rooted by `infrastructure.domain.ts`, holds 35 subdirs, 26 `package.json` and 26 `*.workspace-package.ts` pages beneath it, and 21 loose `*.domain.ts` pages at its top. `akasha/alan/` and `akasha/browser/` are the same shape. Against them, `akasha/seat-system/`, `akasha/code-system/` and `akasha/checks/` are each rooted by their own `*.workspace-package.ts` and hold one manifest apiece — seat-system has 64 subdirs and exactly one `package.json`. For all three container folders, `find -maxdepth 1 -type f ! -name '*.domain.ts'` answers nothing, so no loose code is at the top of any of them.\\n\\nWHICH SUBPATHS A MANIFEST OWES. `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/package-reached-where-named/package-reached-where-named.code-check.code.ts` line 179 exempts an importer inside the package (`if (within(held.folder, path)) continue`) before it compares against the exports map, and the page beside it says 'A file inside a package reaches its siblings directly' at 46-48. So only a module reached from outside owes an entry, and an intra-package relative import owes none. That agrees with the sixteen modules already in `@akasha/ci-benchmark`, which reach each other as `../<slug>/<slug>.module.code.ts`.\\n\\nTHE CARRY. The five files landed as modules of `@akasha/ci-benchmark` at `akasha/infrastructure/ci-benchmark/<slug>/<slug>.module.code.ts` with a `.module.ts` page beside each, five new entries in that package's exports, five new `partSlugs` on `ci-benchmark.workspace-package.ts`, and four dependencies added (`@akasha/ci-containers`, `@akasha/git`, `@akasha/k8s-types`, `@akasha/pages-system`). The holder, `akasha/infrastructure/commands/pipeline-benchmark/pipeline-benchmark.command.code.ts`, moved four specifiers from `@tools/lib/benchmark-*` to `@akasha/ci-benchmark/benchmark-*`; its package `@akasha/pipeline-commands` already named `@akasha/ci-benchmark`, so no manifest of its own changed. Commit 901cad0347, ablation 83a76bc7d8.\\n\\nWHAT SAYS IT WAS RIGHT. `benchmark-job.ts` line 28 already named its runner as `akasha/infrastructure/ci-benchmark/benchmark-running/benchmark-running.module.code.ts`, and all five files already imported `@akasha/ci-benchmark` subpaths. The destination was named in the code before anybody asked where it was.\\n\\nPROOF. A resolution probe over the five new subpaths answered five values, the holder module loaded, and four control arms refused as they must: two `.ts`-suffixed spellings, one absent subpath, and `@tools/lib/benchmark-job` once the originals were gone. `akasha typecheck --seeded` over all twelve changed akasha files answered one diagnostic each, and the clean run answered zero over twelve files judged — scoped to those files, so the folder is unjudged until an audit. Per file, the original body put through the same rewrite and the same `formattedBody` the landing applies is byte-identical to what is on disk, five of five; the control arm with a line seeded in differs five of five.",
+    "Measured 2026-09-03 while carrying the five `tools/lib/benchmark-*.ts` files in.\\n\\nTHE RULE. `akasha/code-system/workspace-packages/workspace-package.page-type.ts` lines 56-66 carry a directive of kind `rule` named Earn The Manifest, act 'Make a folder a workspace package only when a tool must read what it needs', warrant 'A manifest costs upkeep on every change and buys nothing until something reads it', and among its aids 'Importing it by name is not a tool reading it'. Line 15 has `extendsSlug: 'page-type/domain'` and the invariant at 36-38 reads 'A domain becomes a workspace package by stating a manifest', so what decides is which page is at the folder root, not how deep the folder is.\\n\\nTHE COUNTS. `akasha/infrastructure/` is rooted by `infrastructure.domain.ts`, holds 35 subdirs, 26 `package.json` and 26 `*.workspace-package.ts` pages beneath it, and 21 loose `*.domain.ts` pages at its top. `akasha/alan/` and `akasha/browser/` are the same shape. Against them, `akasha/seat-system/`, `akasha/code-system/` and `akasha/checks/` are each rooted by their own `*.workspace-package.ts` and hold one manifest apiece — seat-system has 64 subdirs and exactly one `package.json`. For all three container folders, `find -maxdepth 1 -type f ! -name '*.domain.ts'` answers nothing, so no loose code is at the top of any of them.\\n\\nWHICH SUBPATHS A MANIFEST OWES. `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/package-reached-where-named/package-reached-where-named.code-check.code.ts` line 179 exempts an importer inside the package (`if (within(held.folder, path)) continue`) before it compares against the exports map, and the page beside it says 'A file inside a package reaches its siblings directly' at 46-48. So only a module reached from outside owes an entry, and an intra-package relative import owes none. That agrees with the sixteen modules already in `@akasha/ci-benchmark`, which reach each other as `../<slug>/<slug>.module.code.ts`.\\n\\nTHE CARRY. The five files landed as modules of `@akasha/ci-benchmark` at `akasha/infrastructure/ci-benchmark/<slug>/<slug>.module.code.ts` with a `.module.ts` page beside each, five new entries in that package's exports, five new `partSlugs` on `ci-benchmark.workspace-package.ts`, and four dependencies added (`@akasha/ci-containers`, `@akasha/git`, `@akasha/k8s-types`, `@akasha/pages`). The holder, `akasha/infrastructure/commands/pipeline-benchmark/pipeline-benchmark.command.code.ts`, moved four specifiers from `@tools/lib/benchmark-*` to `@akasha/ci-benchmark/benchmark-*`; its package `@akasha/pipeline-commands` already named `@akasha/ci-benchmark`, so no manifest of its own changed. Commit 901cad0347, ablation 83a76bc7d8.\\n\\nWHAT SAYS IT WAS RIGHT. `benchmark-job.ts` line 28 already named its runner as `akasha/infrastructure/ci-benchmark/benchmark-running/benchmark-running.module.code.ts`, and all five files already imported `@akasha/ci-benchmark` subpaths. The destination was named in the code before anybody asked where it was.\\n\\nPROOF. A resolution probe over the five new subpaths answered five values, the holder module loaded, and four control arms refused as they must: two `.ts`-suffixed spellings, one absent subpath, and `@tools/lib/benchmark-job` once the originals were gone. `akasha typecheck --seeded` over all twelve changed akasha files answered one diagnostic each, and the clean run answered zero over twelve files judged — scoped to those files, so the folder is unjudged until an audit. Per file, the original body put through the same rewrite and the same `formattedBody` the landing applies is byte-identical to what is on disk, five of five; the control arm with a line seeded in differs five of five.",
